\section{Epistemological Framework for Scientific Validation}

\subsection{Scientific Knowledge as Convergent Approximation}

Scientific models achieve legitimacy through convergent empirical validation rather
than deductive certainty. Let $\mathcal{M}$ denote a physical model and $\mathcal{D}$
a set of empirical observations. Validation requires:
\begin{itemize}
\item Consistency with all available data.
\item Predictive reliability for unobserved phenomena.
\item Falsifiability under controlled experimental conditions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Hierarchy of Evidence}

We formalize the hierarchy of scientific evidence as:
\[
\text{Tier 1} \rightarrow \text{Tier 2} \rightarrow \text{Tier 3} \rightarrow 
\text{Tier 4} \rightarrow \text{Consensus Theory},
\]
where:
\begin{description}
\item[Tier 1:] Mechanistic hypothesis and mathematical formulation.
\item[Tier 2:] Controlled laboratory experimentation.
\item[Tier 3:] Statistical significance ($3\sigma$--$5\sigma$).
\item[Tier 4:] Independent replication and peer review.
\end{description}

